# Lean formalization

The project contains no `sorry` or `admit`. If a proof is not short, leave the ledger tag and say so in the journal.

- Lake package name is `balanced-ternary-formal`.
- `formal/Automata/` is a placeholder. Do not invent proofs there.
- Generic theorems live in `BTCalculus/` or `Core/` / `Representation/` / `Operators/`. Collatz-only theorems live under `Problems/Collatz/`. Namespaces match those modules (`Core.Basic`, `Representation.Words`, `Operators.*`, `Problems.Collatz`). Do not add a `CollatzDual` re-export layer or namespace.
- Prefer packaging lemmas that already compile (`newtonStratum_n2` wraps `depthDeficit_n2_visibility`) over rebuilding layer-by-layer.
- Empty `lean` in the ledger is allowed only when the tag is **not** `EXACT — LEAN VERIFIED`.
- Retag to Lean verified only when the Lean statement covers the English statement.
- If that covering statement is a halt or no-nontrivial-cycle theorem, name it. Do not keep the anti-overclaim disclaimer on a matching theorem.
- Build the active graph with `python tools/lab.py build`; use `--include-archive`
  for the historical library. Add `$env:USERPROFILE\.elan\bin` on Windows if needed.
- Before adding a declaration, read `.cursor/skills/add-lean-theorem/SKILL.md` and run `python tools/formalpedia.py search`.
- Follow `docs/architecture/lean_discovery.md`: mathematical namespaces, Mathlib naming,
  and docstrings for new public declarations. Run `python tools/lean_style.py`; do not
  expand the reviewed legacy baseline just to silence a new violation.
- Use fully qualified names in new catalogue references. Inspect complete hypotheses
  with `python tools/formalpedia.py show`; ambiguous short names are candidates, not resolved references.
